// ============================================================================

// ---- WHO IS ACTUALLY AT THE GATE ---------------------------------------------------------------
// MY EXCHANGE RULE [TABLE]. The event says WHAT happened. This says WHO it happened with, and I
// keyed it by REGION, because that's what a region is: not a difficulty setting, a cast list.
//
// The problem in one sentence: a human refugee at the gate in Avernus is nonsense. What arrives in
// Hell is a soul that slipped its chain, or a deserter from the Blood War with lemures on his heels.
// Same event, same mechanic, same weight — a completely different thing at the door. Turning the
// raider dial up does not make a place feel like a place; the people do.
//
// TWO AXES, LAYERED, NOT MULTIPLIED — the third time this pattern has earned its keep (see
// FURNISHING_LADDER's slot@form, and PUB_TAPS by form):
//   BASTION_SLICE_OF_LIFE is keyed by FORM. A quiet week is about the HOUSE — a cavern's nothing-week
//     reads differently from a manor's, and that is a fact about the building.
//   EVENT_CAST is keyed by REGION. Who is at the gate is about the COUNTRY, and no fact about your
//     architecture changes who lives in the hills outside it.
//   Where they meet, region wins, because it is about the visitor and not the door.
//
// SPARSE, AND DELIBERATELY SO. 17 regions x 13 events x 12 rows is 2,652 strings, and 2,652 mediocre
// lines are worse than 300 good ones. Ten of the seventeen regions are the SAME CAST — humans and
// their neighbours, on a road, in the Realms. A refugee in Cormyr and a refugee in the Dalelands is
// a farmer whose barn burned. Only the seven that are not the Realms need their own people:
//   the Underdark · Barovia · Chult · Avernus · the Feywild · Wildspace · Icewind Dale
// And only seven of the thirteen events are about the COUNTRY at all — Criminal Hireling, Lost
// Hirelings and Treasure are about your household, and a thief is a thief in any weather.
//
//   "event"          the default. Every Realms region reads it.
//   "event@region"   an override, for a country whose people are not those people.
