// Who is at the gate this week. Region first, because the visitor is the country's, not the house's.
// Whitelist: no cast for this event in this country falls to the default; no default returns null and
// the event's own note stands, which is how the ten Realms regions and any homebrew place work.

// Seized. Below is what the DMG says seizing costs and buys.
// ---- WHAT IS ACTUALLY AT THE FAIR --------------------------------------------------------------
// EXCHANGE RULE [TABLE]. A d20, not a d12 — a fair has more in it than a quiet week does, and the
// table should be able to surprise a player who has thrown a dozen of them.
//
// ONE FEATURE PER ROOM. The same per-room mechanism the events use: a bigger house throws a bigger
// fair, and "bigger" means MORE THINGS HAPPENING, not one thing costing more. A one-room steading
// gets one feature and a six-room hall gets six, and the six-room hall's fair is a different EVENT,
// not the same event with a bigger number on it.
//
// AND THE TAKE IS IN THE FEATURE. Each row carries its own `takes` — how many rolls on the DMG's
// Treasure table that part of the fair generates. That is where the swing lives: it is not a
// modifier bolted onto a flat number, it is the fact that a good stall earns and a bad one doesn't.
// Reuses resolveTreasure, which is already what the fair's take is made of. Nothing new.
//   Realms   every feature takes 2. A fair is a fair; some are better, none are ruinous.
//   AVERNUS  0 to 5. A six-room fair in Hell is anywhere from nothing at all to thirty rolls, and
//            you will not know which until it is over. That is not a difficulty setting; that is
//            what a contract with a devil IS.
//   BAROVIA  1 to 3, always. Nothing here is ever quite a disaster and nothing is ever quite good.
//            The veneer holds. That is the horror.
//
// WHERE THE VOICES COME FROM, and it is worth writing down because it is a deliberate blend:
//   HISTORY   — the medieval fair is the spine. Bryene's New Year fed three hundred; a village
//               harvest-home was a barrel and a fiddle. Feast days, guild pageants, hiring fairs,
//               the piepowder courts that settled disputes before the dust was off your feet.
//   THE REALMS — the Witchlight Carnival is the tone to reach for: wonder with a price on it, and
//               everyone at the gate slightly too pleased to see you.
//   AVERNUS   — two references, and they are the same note from opposite ends. Rakdos, of Ravnica:
//               the performance IS the murder and the audience knows and applauds. And Wishmaster's
//               Djinn: you get PRECISELY what you asked for, and it destroys you, and it was
//               technically a gift. A Hell fair delivers everything it promised. That is the joke.
//   BAROVIA   — the feast that is a stage set. Everyone plays their part beautifully. The wine is
//               real, the laughter is not, and everybody has done this before.
